Use kubectl + psql inside the Postgres pod.

Quick way (inside DB pod)
If you used the manifests as created (deployment name db-deployment, db dispatchdb, user root):

kubectl exec -it deploy/db-deployment -- psql -U root -d dispatchdb
Then run SQL:

\dt
SELECT * FROM submissions ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM submissions;
Exit with:

\q
One-liner (no interactive shell)
kubectl exec -it deploy/db-deployment -- psql -U root -d dispatchdb -c "SELECT id,name,email,message,created_at FROM submissions ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20;"
If you use a namespace
Add -n <namespace> to all commands, for example:

kubectl -n dispatch exec -it deploy/db-deployment -- psql -U root -d dispatchdb -c "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM submissions;"
If deployment name differs, find the DB pod first
kubectl get pods
kubectl get pods -l app=dispatch-db
Then:

kubectl exec -it <db-pod-name> -- psql -U root -d dispatchdb
If you want, I can also give you commands to verify persistence after restart (delete db pod -> check rows still exist).
